year={1990},
annote={The authors have conducted a large-scale experiment in
N-version programming. A total of 27 versions of a
program were prepared independently from the same
specification at two universities. The results of
executing the versions revealed that the versions were
individually extremely reliable but that the number of
input cases in which more than one failed was
substantially more than would be expected if they were
statistically independent. After the versions had been
